WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:01.418
So the other day I was watching

00:00:01.418 --> 00:00:02.293
Shang-Chi and I was

00:00:05.088 --> 00:00:07.173
Oh my God, why did you do that?

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:09.759
I just didn't like the way 
you delivered those lines.

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:12.000
So you punched me in the 
freaking face?

00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:15.000
So, the other day

00:00:17.866 --> 00:00:18.557
So, the

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:21.312
So— 

00:00:22.731 --> 00:00:23.982
So the other day

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:25.442
I was watching Shang-Chi

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:27.612
and I was so impressed by the 
fight sequence on the bus

00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:29.112
and how they were able 
to make it feel

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:32.198
so visceral and real, without actually
hurting anybody.

00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:35.493
I know the punches and kicks aren't real, 
but they feel real.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.120
And I wanted to know 
how they did it.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.663
So I made a call.

00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:41.416
I'm a second unit director 
and stunt coordinator.

00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:43.168
That's Wade Eastwood.

00:00:43.168 --> 00:00:45.670
No relation to other Hollywood Eastwoods.

00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:48.135
He's worked on some of the 
great action movies

00:00:48.135 --> 00:00:49.215
of the past decade

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:52.385
and you can see him right there in 2008’s

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.971
Quantum of Solace as 
Daniel Craig's stunt double.

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:57.599
Before Wade even takes 
a step on set...

00:00:57.849 --> 00:01:01.269
he and his team will do a ton of prep
and previsualization.

00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:04.230
You might be familiar with VFX 
pre-vis thanks to Marvel.

00:01:04.647 --> 00:01:06.320
These are the lo-fi animations

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.109
they do in advance of 
making the movie.

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:09.402
But oftentimes

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.780
—stunt teams will do... 
—What I call the action vis.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.865
I shoot it live with the proper cameras.

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:16.242
Action vis often looks more like this.

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:19.788
Sets are made of boxes and 
stuntmen play the lead roles

00:01:19.788 --> 00:01:21.456
as they figure out all the action

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:23.833
and the camera angles 
in a safe space.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.960
Then when it comes time to 
shoot the movie...

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.546
The main unit would shoot the drama 
and the acting scenes,

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:33.426
so then I would shoot everything 
action-wise with my unit.

00:01:33.718 --> 00:01:36.179
Let's break a fight down 
to its most basic element...

00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:37.597
like a punch.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:38.556
How do you sell a good

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:40.806
Both sides have to work

00:01:40.806 --> 00:01:42.102
the A-side and the B side.

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:44.562
If the person throwing 
a stunt punch throws it with a

00:01:44.562 --> 00:01:47.023
you know, the best movement and 
performance you can have

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:49.859
but the person receiving
it does a really lame reaction...

00:01:50.026 --> 00:01:50.735
It doesn't work.

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:52.904
Both parties have to give 100%.

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:55.782
This is important because 
the actors and the stunt people

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:58.034
generally aren't actually 
hitting each other.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:01:59.869
Which can look fake

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:01.454
if you're not "stacking the action"

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:04.415
which is the cool industry term 
for saying that

00:02:04.415 --> 00:02:06.793
the people and the punching 
need to be layered

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:08.586
in a way that cheats the camera.

00:02:08.586 --> 00:02:11.381
If we're not stacking the action...

00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:13.675
Obviously you can see that 
there’s a distance, it's not a sell.

00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:16.636
If I'm here suddenly, and you do 
a punch and I react

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:18.680
it can come across.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.390
So we have the same gap.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.766
It's just where we put the camera.

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:22.851
It's a couple of inches.

00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:26.020
It's, you know, maybe a foot max, depending on what it is.

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:28.305
Sometimes, though, as Michael B. Jordan

00:02:28.305 --> 00:02:29.983
explained on the Graham Norton Show

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.694
you have to actually make contact 
to sell it.

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:34.779
When you do slo-mo shots, 
you can't fake it.

00:02:34.779 --> 00:02:37.574
You would see the space and it just
wouldn't connect.

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:40.910
This is Michael Jordan taking a real punch:

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:42.453
“And...action!”

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:47.083
The B side of a hit,

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:50.336
AKA the person getting hit, 
has all the power

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.630
in terms of selling how hard 
a punch should be.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:56.384
So a small hit might 
leave me unfazed.

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:57.732
But if I get hit

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:03.641
so if I fall to the ground, it looks 
a lot more powerful.

00:03:03.766 --> 00:03:05.894
But knowing how to fall correctly

00:03:05.894 --> 00:03:08.730
and safely is a whole skill on its own.

00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:10.481
If you watch a big car crash

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:13.443
the kinetic energy is breaking down
throughout the whole move.

00:03:13.568 --> 00:03:15.320
If that car hits a wall 
and stops dead

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:16.571
the driver inside would be dead

00:03:16.571 --> 00:03:18.573
because your body cannot 
withstand that much impact.

00:03:18.656 --> 00:03:19.991
And that's really the thing with falling.

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:21.784
You've got to just do just enough.

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:24.787
The forearm might just absorb a little bit

00:03:24.787 --> 00:03:27.128
before the shoulder does, 
before the back does

00:03:27.128 --> 00:03:28.458
before the whole body does.

00:03:28.458 --> 00:03:30.919
And it's 5%, 5%, 5%.

00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:33.504
And making sure that you make it 
look really gnarly

00:03:33.504 --> 00:03:35.924
but you're actually... it's not that gnarly

00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:38.426
although obviously sometimes 
you are going to hurt yourself,

00:03:38.426 --> 00:03:40.678
bruise yourself, pop a rib. 
You are going to do something.

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:44.766
But that can be mitigated with 
strategically hidden padding...

00:03:45.016 --> 00:03:49.062
which you can see right... here, 
in this scene from Atomic Blonde.

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:50.647
Though these stairs are supposed to
look like

00:03:50.647 --> 00:03:52.065
marble or concrete

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:54.609
this bend indicates that it's a much safer

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:55.944
and softer material.

00:03:55.944 --> 00:03:58.274
I use quite a lot of soft materials
where I can.

00:03:58.274 --> 00:04:00.365
I’ll have our art department 
make something

00:04:00.365 --> 00:04:02.951
look exactly like wood or concrete, 
whatever it is.

00:04:02.951 --> 00:04:06.287
And if we didn't have the padding, 
we'd do one or two takes.

00:04:06.287 --> 00:04:07.455
It would be really painful.

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:10.124
And you'd probably wouldn't 
hit it as hard.

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:13.503
So we put a little bit of high density 
absorption in there

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:16.589
and you can hit it much harder
and you can do multiple takes.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:17.966
And for that brief moment,

00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:20.301
the audience get to live that 
hard impact with you.

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:23.638
And once that fight goes into 
post-production

00:04:23.638 --> 00:04:26.266
there are even more tricks used 
to make it look like

00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:28.977
the hits are just a little bit harder.

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:31.145
Like, that hit looked pretty hard. 

00:04:31.145 --> 00:04:34.774
But if you cut out the frame where the fist is supposed to make contact,

00:04:34.983 --> 00:04:37.610
your brain will fill in the gap and it'll look like

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.154
there's just a bit more power to that hit.

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:42.824
You can see this trick used in this shot
from Shang-Chi

00:04:42.824 --> 00:04:46.035
where Simu Liu’s elbow 
pops on impact.

00:04:46.077 --> 00:04:48.746
You can also do things where 
visual effects can slightly,

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.604
we don't do it, but a lot of movies do,

00:04:50.604 --> 00:04:52.458
where you can extend the fist

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.252
and actually make it hit the face.

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:55.795
You can see a good example of this

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.714
in this clip from No Time to Die,

00:04:57.714 --> 00:05:01.468
where the VFX team gave 
Ana de Armas digital legs

00:05:01.468 --> 00:05:03.428
and they pulled the stunt guy in slightly closer

00:05:03.428 --> 00:05:05.888
to get more energy out of the hit.

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:07.515
And of course, the sound design

00:05:07.515 --> 00:05:09.225
of a fight scene can add clarity

00:05:09.225 --> 00:05:12.186
as to when a swing misses or hits.

00:05:13.354 --> 00:05:14.564
And if you took the sound out

00:05:14.564 --> 00:05:15.988
of the scene completely...

00:05:16.441 --> 00:05:18.067
you wouldn't as easily be able to tell

00:05:18.067 --> 00:05:20.320
how softened a blocked kick feels...

00:05:20.945 --> 00:05:24.032
or how bone shatteringly hard 
a punch feels.

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:27.368
But no matter how cool a fight looks,

00:05:27.368 --> 00:05:30.580
it won't feel exciting if there's no stakes.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.458
While I love the Avengers movies, 
some of the scenes

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:36.113
where they're just tearing through an army of bad guys

00:05:36.113 --> 00:05:38.129
kind of washes over me a little bit.

00:05:38.254 --> 00:05:40.256
But then you have something like 
Back to the Future

00:05:40.256 --> 00:05:43.468
where the whole movie builds to 
this one punch

00:05:43.468 --> 00:05:45.762
that changes the course of history.

00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:47.889
There's a lot of fights that are out there
that are, you know,

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:49.307
great spectacles of fights.

00:05:49.307 --> 00:05:51.017
But if you look at why they're fighting

00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:52.268
or what it's about, 
it's not

00:05:52.268 --> 00:05:53.811
there's not really any rhyme or reason.

00:05:53.811 --> 00:05:55.188
But when it's a proper fight,

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:57.899
a proper journey, a proper, you know, 
bit of emotion

00:05:57.899 --> 00:06:00.276
that is when a fight feels exciting.

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:02.278
That is

00:06:02.278 --> 00:06:04.575
WHEN a fight feels exciting.

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:07.992
That's when a FIGHT feels exciting.

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:09.786
How was that?

00:06:09.786 --> 00:06:10.672
Oh, God, no!

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.208
What were you impressed by? Huh?

00:06:15.504 --> 00:06:16.004
[laughter]

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.671
I was watching Shang-Chi...

00:06:21.172 --> 00:06:23.883
I was looking you too dead in the eyes.
[laughs]

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:25.496
...hitting each other.

00:06:26.925 --> 00:06:30.223
Sorry, I forgot that I was supposed to punch. 

00:06:31.307 --> 00:06:34.102
Sorry I’m laughing! You can’t see me right?

00:06:34.102 --> 00:06:34.769
Right?

00:06:35.144 --> 00:06:36.062
Was that like, too?

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:37.438
I feel like that was maybe too silly.

00:06:40.274 --> 00:06:41.943
Cool, right?

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:43.559
I think that's a wrap.

